-- Assertions about Pong, in the same language Pong is written in.
--
-- Every one of these runs on every console. That is only possible because the
-- assertions are written in the same relative vocabulary as the game: `centery`
-- is 9 on a Game Boy and 14 on a Mega Drive, and `expect ball1.y > centery`
-- means the same thing on both.

test the title screen waits for a button
play 2 seconds
expect scene title

test pressing a starts the game
press a
expect scene play

test the ball serves down and to the left
press a
play 0.5 seconds
expect ball1.y > centery
expect ball1.x < centerx

test the player paddle reaches the left wall and stops there
press a
hold left for 5 seconds
expect paddle1.x = 0
expect paddle1.xdirection = 0

test the player paddle reaches the right wall and stops there
press a
hold right for 5 seconds
expect paddle1.right = screenwidth
expect paddle1.xdirection = 0

-- The ball reaches the bottom wall at almost exactly the same tick on every
-- console (49 on a Game Boy, 51 on a Mega Drive) because speeds are relative.
-- That is the balance work, asserted rather than assumed.
test a still player concedes a point and the ball returns to the middle
press a
play 0.92 seconds
expect score2.value = 1
expect abs(ball1.y - centery) < 15vh
expect abs(ball1.x - centerx) < 15vw

test the opponent tracks the ball
press a
play 3.33 seconds
expect abs(paddle2.centerx - ball1.centerx) < 10vw

test the opponent stays inside the court
press a
play 10 seconds
expect paddle2.x >= 0
expect paddle2.right <= screenwidth

-- The opponent's handicap, asserted rather than described. `aim` is redrawn
-- every time the ball leaves the player's paddle, and a non-zero aim is what
-- stops the return coming back down the same line every rally.
test the opponent aims off-centre once a rally has started
press a
play 3.33 seconds
expect abs(aim.value) > 0
